    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Prior research has suggested that young children have a strong bias to categorize based on appearance. This is best demonstrated by elementary children’s performance on Frank Keil’s classic transformation task. Children were presented with scenarios where an entity underwent an operation that made it look radically different. When asking what the entity was after the changes, 5-6-year-old children overwhelmingly relied on its new appearance. It was until 4th grade that children were more likely to judge that the entity retained its original membership, despite its new appearance. Keil found this shift in category judgments only for animals, and not for objects where they instead, regardless of age, almost always went with the new appearance of the objects. Here we ask whether children will use a thing’s purpose when deciding what it is. We found that mentioning that a thing’s purpose remained the same before and after the changes led children to rely less on its appearance when judging what it is. Interestingly, this was true for both animals and objects. We take these findings to put pressure on one dominant view in the literature, what we call scientific essentialism. For the past 30 years, researchers have argued that children reason about natural kinds, but not artifacts, in terms of what makes them the kind of thing they are, their essence. But these essences are represented as placeholders. When they are elaborated, which is rare, they are elaborated with scientific information. In light of our findings, we suggest that placeholders might instead be elaborated in terms of what something is for. And since we associate what something is with what it is for, both artifacts and animals are essentialized. In short, our data suggests that children view what something is for as essential for what something is.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
